WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:02.740
The premise of the project

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.960
is that economists are too pompous.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.220
One reason we don't get past

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.820
the first sentence is that people shut their ears

00:00:12.825 --> 00:00:14.555
because they know they've heard these words.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.140
Many of the things economists say are probably

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.300
relevant and useful, but we say it in ways

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.760
that put off people.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.400
Many miles away in the southwest corner of the city

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.740
in a circumscribed neighborhood

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.200
Monty ponders under a dry tap.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.600
Sarnath really is an economist,

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.520
he's just managed to put a number of layers

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:40.740
around him so that people don't

00:00:40.745 --> 00:00:43.305
immediately run away when they see him

00:00:43.925 --> 00:00:46.385
The inhabitants of the city of Gates

00:00:46.385 --> 00:00:48.775
are linked together by their common dream

00:00:49.975 --> 00:00:52.185
that of possessing an extra bedroom.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.780
Abhijit knows, clearly, about greed.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.140
No, he's not greedy

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:01.540
I'm very greedy

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.040
But he knows the general... he's

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:05.540
a blackbelt in economics.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.420
With this grant, Abhijit and I am collaborating

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:10.920
to make an insubordinate theatrical lecture

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.600
in which Abhijit speaks about certain

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.940
guiding principles to understand water

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.340
water crisis, water management.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.020
The lectures are about water

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.480
but water is a context for the intersection

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.040
of any number of forces in economics.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:33.440
Globalization, greed, rainfall,

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.385
climate change, everything hits water.

00:01:36.385 --> 00:01:39.060
So water is a wonderful context for

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.440
bringing together any number of these

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.100
key ideas in economics.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.900
With this series of lectures,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.180
which have humor, imagination

00:01:51.185 --> 00:01:54.145
warmth, explanation, and a rich

00:01:54.145 --> 00:01:56.660
cast of characters who appear,

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.820
as I said, and then reappear

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.480
as if it's like a soap opera being told,

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.020
but by an economist.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.180
Art is how we construct new languages

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:14.160
so in a sense, that's at the core of what we're

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.980
trying to do. We're trying to construct a new language

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.420
for saying the same things.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.180
New languages have the function

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:23.700
that they wake us up.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.500
I think that's the... if we get there,

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.360
that would be success.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.260
If we woke up people.

